# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DEUCE — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local for development, and set the same names in the Vercel
# project for production. .env.local is gitignored; never commit a real key.
#
# Every one of these is validated at boot by src/env.ts. A missing or
# placeholder value fails the build rather than producing a half-working
# deployment.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Supabase ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dashboard → Project Settings → API.
#
# The PUBLISHABLE key is designed to be public: it identifies the project and
# authorises nothing. Access is decided by row-level security against the
# caller's session, so shipping it to the browser is the design.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://your-project.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="sb_publishable_..."

# The SECRET key bypasses row-level security completely. Server-side only, and
# it must never appear in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable. Deuce uses it for exactly one
# thing: deleting an account, which the account itself can no longer authorise.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY="sb_secret_..."

# ── The site's own address ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where emailed sign-in links come back to. It must match a redirect URL
# configured in Supabase Auth, exactly — including the scheme and any port.
#
# In local development use localhost (not 127.0.0.1) for BOTH this and the
# Supabase site_url: PKCE keeps its code verifier in a cookie, and those two
# hostnames are different cookie origins, so a flow that starts on one and
# finishes on the other silently produces no session.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://deuce.example.com"

# ── Upstash Redis ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION, optional locally.
#
# It rate-limits the sign-in endpoint, which sends email to other people's
# mailboxes at an address that is guessable from a name. A counter in process
# memory is not a rate limit when the platform runs several instances, which is
# the normal state of a Vercel deployment — so the app refuses to start in
# production without these. Locally, `next dev` is one process and the in-memory
# limiter in src/lib/rate-limit.ts is a real limit.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=""
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=""

# ── Operator identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED BEFORE LAUNCH. GDPR art. 13 obliges you to say who is responsible for
# people's data and how to reach them, and these values are printed on the
# privacy and terms pages. Left unset, those pages say plainly that they are not
# configured — which is honest, and not something to publish.
APP_OPERATOR_NAME=""
APP_OPERATOR_ADDRESS=""
APP_CONTACT_EMAIL=""
